WN.lb(30,{"mt_SeNxOZTHCN":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Tomorrow is the school play. Lily worries she will forget her lines. Which word fits?","choices":["Anxious","Frustrated","Excited"],"answer":0,"hint":"She is worried about what might happen. Which word means that?","explain":"Anxious means worried about what might happen. Lily is worried about tomorrow."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which breathing helps you calm down?","choices":["Slow breaths in and out","Fast, quick breaths","Holding your breath"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the balloon. Fast or slow?","explain":"Slow breaths in and out settle your body. Fast breaths keep you wound up."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Sam takes Ben's ball on purpose and will not give it back. Ben feels…","choices":["Angry","Disappointed","Sleepy"],"answer":0,"hint":"Someone did something unfair on purpose. Which word fits?","explain":"Angry fits when someone does something unfair. Disappointed is for a hope that did not come true."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which one is a safe strategy for a big feeling?","choices":["Walk away for a bit","Kick the wall","Shout at a friend"],"answer":0,"hint":"A strategy helps you cope without hurting anyone.","explain":"Walking away for a bit gives you time to calm down. Kicking or shouting can hurt or make it worse."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"You must wait {n} minutes for your turn. Waiting calmly is called…","choices":["Patience","Pushing in","Giving up"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which word means waiting in a calm way?","explain":"Patience means you wait in a calm way, even for {n} minutes.","params":{"n":[2,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"What does cope mean?","choices":["Get through a hard feeling safely","Hide a feeling forever","Feel happy all the time"],"answer":0,"hint":"Strategies help you do this when a feeling is big.","explain":"To cope is to get through a hard feeling safely. You still have the feeling, but you handle it."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Zoe has {n} jobs to do at once and feels overwhelmed. What helps her settle?","choices":["Do one job at a time","Do all {n} at once, fast","Add one more job"],"answer":0,"hint":"Overwhelmed means too much at once. How can she make it less?","explain":"One job at a time makes the pile feel smaller, so the feeling settles.","params":{"n":[4,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Why is it good to know the exact word for a feeling?","choices":["It helps you know what to do","It makes the feeling go away at once","It is not useful"],"answer":0,"hint":"Naming a feeling is the first step. The first step to what?","explain":"The exact word helps you and others know what will help, such as a break for overwhelmed or a hug for disappointed."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Ella's drawing keeps going wrong. She has tried {n} times. Which word fits best?","choices":["Frustrated","Sleepy","Disappointed"],"answer":0,"hint":"She keeps trying and it keeps going wrong. Which word means that?","explain":"Frustrated means you try and try, but it keeps going wrong. That is what happened {n} times.","params":{"n":[3,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"The pool is shut today. Max was looking forward to it. Which word fits best?","choices":["Disappointed","Angry","Overwhelmed"],"answer":0,"hint":"Did someone do something unfair, or did a hope not come true?","explain":"Max hoped to swim and it did not happen, so he is disappointed. Nobody was unfair to him."},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"You feel cross. You count slowly to {n}. What is counting?","choices":["A strategy","A feeling","A game you win"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is counting a feeling, or a plan you use?","explain":"Counting to {n} is a strategy: a plan you can use to cope with a big feeling.","params":{"n":[5,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Jay has to tidy up, do homework, feed the cat and get dressed, all right now. How might he feel?","choices":["Overwhelmed","Calm","Disappointed"],"answer":0,"hint":"Lots of things all at once. Which word means too much?","explain":"Overwhelmed means too much, all at once. Doing one job at a time can help Jay settle."}],"explain":{"kid":["Some feelings are big. Saying I feel bad does not tell much. A big feeling has its own word.","Frustrated is when you try and try, but it keeps going wrong. Disappointed is when you hoped for a thing, and it did not happen.","Anxious is when you worry about what might happen. Overwhelmed is when there is too much, all at once.","Then you can calm down. Breathe in slowly and out slowly. A strategy is a plan, like count to ten. It helps you cope. Patience means you wait in a calm way."],"adult":["This topic gives children the words for strong feelings and for handling them: frustrated, disappointed, anxious, overwhelmed, calm, breathe, strategy, cope, settle and patience. Studies of emotional granularity suggest that people who name feelings precisely handle them better, and putting a feeling into words can make it less intense. The word is the first tool.","The lab works with each word in action. Children match four scenes to the exact feeling, sort cases into angry or disappointed, pile jobs into a bag until a meter shows overwhelmed and then settle it one job at a time, slow their breathing with a balloon, and choose safe strategies for a calm-down toolbox.","Common mistakes come up often. Children use one catch-all word, such as mad or bad, for every strong feeling. They mix up angry, which usually comes from something that feels unfair, with disappointed, which comes from a hope that did not happen. They breathe fast and shallow when told to take a breath. And they treat hitting, shouting or throwing as ways to calm down.","Name your own feelings out loud with these words, for example: I am frustrated because the jar will not open, so I will take a breath and try again. When your child is calm, ask which word fits a time they were upset. Keep a short list of their own strategies on the fridge."]},"familyTip":"When your child is upset, offer two exact words to choose from: are you frustrated or disappointed? Once they pick one, ask which strategy from their list they want to try first.","keyIdea":"Big feelings have exact words: frustrated, disappointed, anxious and overwhelmed. Naming the feeling is the first step to handling it. Calm-down words name what helps: breathe slowly, use a strategy, cope, settle and have patience.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Does it help you calm down? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Helps me calm down","Makes it worse"],"items":[["Slow breaths",0],["Count to ten",0],["Tell a grown-up",0],["Throw things",1],["Shout at someone",1],["Fast breaths",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagbig","caption":"Flagship lab: name big feelings, tell angry from disappointed, do one job at a time, breathe with a balloon and build a calm-down toolbox.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_0VOZSVjo6c":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which of these is most likely to be good stress?","choices":["Nerves before a school play you have practised for","Worry about money at home that lasts months","Being bullied every day"],"answer":0,"hint":"Good stress is short and feels like a stretch you can handle.","explain":"Nerves before a practised play are short and manageable, so they can help you perform. The others last too long or are out of your control."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Zara has felt too stressed to enjoy anything for {n} weeks. What should she do?","choices":["Tell a trusted adult and get help","Keep it to herself and push on","Drink energy drinks to keep going"],"answer":0,"hint":"What is the two-week sign?","explain":"{n} weeks without enjoyment is past the two-week point, so it is time to tell a parent, teacher or doctor.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Why does exercise help with stress?","choices":["It uses up stress hormones and helps you sleep","It only makes you too tired to worry","It does not help at all"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what happens to stress hormones and to sleep.","explain":"Exercise lowers stress hormones like adrenaline and cortisol, and it helps you sleep better, which helps you recover."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"When the stress alarm goes off, what happens to digestion?","choices":["It slows down","It speeds up","It stops for a whole day"],"answer":0,"hint":"Where does the body send its energy when it is ready to act?","explain":"Digestion slows so that blood and energy can go to the muscles. That is why stress can feel like butterflies."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Leo has {k} pieces of homework due and feels swamped. Which step gives him back some control?","choices":["List them and start the smallest one","Leave them all until the night before","Ignore them and play games"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which choice turns one big pressure into small steps?","explain":"Listing all {k} tasks and starting the smallest one turns a big pressure into steps he controls, which makes the stress easier to handle.","params":{"k":[3,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Why can writing your worries down help?","choices":["It gets them out of your head so you can sort them","It makes the problems vanish","It only helps if nobody reads it"],"answer":0,"hint":"What happens to worries that just go round and round in your head?","explain":"Writing worries down moves them out of your head, so you can see them clearly and plan what to do."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which pattern is worst for your body?","choices":["Medium stress that never lets up for weeks","One scary moment on a rollercoaster","Nerves before a race"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one gives the alarm no time to switch off?","explain":"Stress that never lets up keeps the alarm on, which wears down sleep, mood and health. Short scares pass."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which breath calms you down most?","choices":["Slow, with the breath out longer than the breath in","Fast and deep","Holding your breath as long as you can"],"answer":0,"hint":"Fast breathing is what the alarm does.","explain":"A slow breath with a longer out-breath slows the heart. Fast breathing and holding your breath keep you tense."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Before a race, Ana's heart is pounding and her muscles feel tight. What is this?","choices":["Her body's alarm getting her ready to run","A sign she is ill","Proof that stress is always bad"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what adrenaline does to the heart and muscles.","explain":"Adrenaline speeds up the heart and tenses muscles to get the body ready for action. Short stress like this can help her run well."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Ben has been worried about a problem at home for {n} weeks and can hardly sleep. What kind of stress is this?","choices":["Bad stress: it has lasted too long","Good stress: it keeps him alert","Not stress at all"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does the alarm get a chance to switch off?","explain":"{n} weeks of worry with poor sleep means the alarm never switches off. That is bad stress, and he should talk to a trusted adult.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Why does a slow breath help when you feel stressed?","choices":["A long breath out slows your heart down","It makes you fall asleep","It stops stress hormones forever"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which part of the breath should be longer?","explain":"Breathing out slowly, for longer than you breathe in, slows your heart rate and helps the alarm calm down."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Kai has a test in {d} days and feels he can do it if he revises a little each day. What kind of stress is this?","choices":["Good stress: a stretch he can handle with an end in sight","Bad stress: he should skip the test","No stress at all"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is it too big, too long or out of his control?","explain":"A test in {d} days that feels doable, with a plan he controls, is good stress. It can help him focus.","params":{"d":[2,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"After a stressful day, which helps the alarm switch off?","choices":["A walk and a good night's sleep","An energy drink","Scrolling on a phone until 1 am"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which choice helps you recover and sleep?","explain":"Moving your body uses up stress hormones and sleep lets them fall back. Caffeine and late screens keep the alarm on."}],"explain":{"kid":["Stress is your body's alarm. When something feels hard or scary, your brain sends out two stress hormones, adrenaline and cortisol. Your heart beats faster. You breathe faster. Your muscles get tight. Your stomach slows down, which is the butterflies feeling. Your body is getting ready to fight, run or freeze.","Some stress is good for you. Nerves before a match or a test can make you sharp and focused. This is good stress when the task is a stretch you can handle, it will end soon, and you have some control. Stress turns bad when it is far too big, when it goes on for weeks, or when you feel stuck with no help.","Your body needs time for the alarm to switch off. Sleep, moving your body and talking to a friend all help it calm down. If stress never stops, the alarm stays on. Then you may sleep badly, get headaches, catch colds more often or feel low. Late-night scrolling and energy drinks keep the alarm on.","Good tools work for real reasons. Exercise uses up stress hormones and helps you sleep. A slow breath out, longer than the breath in, slows your heart. Talking shares the load. Writing worries down gets them out of your head. If you cannot sleep or enjoy things for two weeks or more, tell a trusted adult."],"adult":["This topic helps young people tell helpful stress from harmful stress. Short stress that feels like a manageable challenge often improves focus and performance. Stress that is overwhelming, long lasting or out of the person's control is the kind linked to poor sleep, low mood and illness.","The lab works through five ideas. Learners set off the body's alarm and tap each body part to see what adrenaline does, then turn three dials (size, length and control) to change the same football final from bad stress into good stress. Next they drop recovery into the gaps of a hard week so the stress line can fall back. Then they match coping tools to the reason each one works, and finally they slow a racing heart with long out-breaths.","A common mistake is to think all stress is harmful, so that normal nerves before a test feel like a sign of illness. Another is to judge a coping habit only by how it feels in the moment. Scrolling late at night and energy drinks feel like a break, but they cut sleep and keep the alarm on. Many children also keep stress to themselves for weeks, when two weeks of poor sleep or lost enjoyment is the point to get help.","At home, notice sleep and enjoyment, since both change early when stress builds. Help your child break a big pressure into small steps they can control, and protect time for sleep, exercise and friends during exam season."]},"familyTip":"Before a big event, try the slow breath together: in for four, out for six, three times. Afterwards, ask what helped your child feel ready and what made the nerves worse.","keyIdea":"Stress is your body's alarm. Adrenaline and cortisol make your heart race, your breathing speed up and your muscles tense, ready to act. Short stress that feels like a stretch you can handle, with an end in sight, can help you focus and perform: that is good stress. Stress that is too big, goes on for weeks, or leaves you with no control is bad stress, and it wears down sleep, mood and health. Recovery lets the alarm switch off, and tools like exercise, slow breathing, talking and writing worries down work for real reasons. If stress stops you sleeping or enjoying things for two weeks or more, tell a trusted adult.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Good stress or bad stress? Ask: is it too big, too long, or out of my control?","opts":{"bins":["Good stress","Bad stress"],"items":[["Nerves before a match",0],["A test you have revised for",0],["Giving a short talk in class",0],["Bullying every day",1],["Worry at home for months",1],["Ten deadlines on one night",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagstress","caption":"Flagship lab: set off the body's alarm, dial bad stress into good, let the alarm switch off, and breathe your heart rate down.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_i1kk9HDctI":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What does resilience mean?","choices":["Never feeling upset","Bouncing back after a setback","Pretending things are fine"],"answer":1,"hint":"Resilient people do still get upset.","explain":"Resilience is bouncing back after a setback. You can feel upset and still be resilient."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which is self-talk?","choices":["Mum says, \"Well done.\"","In his head, Ali says, \"Keep going, you can do this.\"","A teacher reads a story aloud"],"answer":1,"hint":"Self-talk happens inside your own head.","explain":"Self-talk is what you say to yourself in your head, like Ali's \"Keep going\"."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which thought shows a fixed mindset?","choices":["I'll never be good at drawing. I just can't.","Drawing is hard for me now, but I'm learning.","I'll ask how to draw hands better."],"answer":0,"hint":"A fixed mindset says you cannot change.","explain":"\"I'll never be good\" says you cannot change, which is fixed mindset. The other two show growth."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Noor is crying so hard she cannot think or speak. What word fits?","choices":["Distress","Persevere","Regulate"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which word names a feeling so big you cannot think?","explain":"Distress is a feeling so big that you cannot think. Regulating would be bringing it down."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Sam's meter is at {n} out of 10. Which tool helps him regulate?","choices":["Shout at the person who laughed","Slow breaths with a long breath out","Keep thinking about it again and again"],"answer":1,"hint":"Which one brings the feeling down instead of up?","explain":"Slow breaths with a long out-breath calm the body, so the meter falls from {n}. Shouting and going over it again push it up.","params":{"n":[6,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Max tried to learn a song for {n} weeks and kept going, practising a new part each day. Which word fits best?","choices":["Persevere","Trigger","Setback"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which word means keeping going when it is hard?","explain":"Keeping going for {n} weeks and trying new parts is persevering.","params":{"n":[2,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which is the best example of resilience?","choices":["Ruby never tries anything hard","Ruby is upset when she loses, then trains and plays again","Ruby says she does not care about losing"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look for falling down and getting back up.","explain":"Being upset and then training and playing again is bouncing back: resilience. Avoiding hard things or not caring is not."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"What is the difference between a trigger and a setback?","choices":["They are the same thing","A trigger sets off a feeling; a setback is something going wrong","A setback is always someone being unkind"],"answer":1,"hint":"One word is about feelings starting; the other is about plans going wrong.","explain":"A trigger sets off a big feeling. A setback is something going wrong that slows you down. One event can be both."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Ella's sandcastle is knocked over by a wave. What is this?","choices":["A trigger","A setback","Resilience"],"answer":1,"hint":"Which word means something going wrong that slows you down for now?","explain":"A setback is something going wrong that slows you down. Ella can build it again further up the beach."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Omar's anger is at {n} out of 10. He breathes out slowly until it drops to 4 and he can think. What did he do?","choices":["He regulated his feeling","He hid his feeling","He gave up"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which word means bringing a feeling down until you can think?","explain":"Bringing the feeling from {n} down to 4, where he can think, is regulating. It did not have to reach zero.","params":{"n":[7,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which thought shows a growth mindset?","choices":["I'm the best at everything!","I can't do fractions yet, but practice will help.","Some people just can't do fractions."],"answer":1,"hint":"Growth mindset is about getting better, not just feeling happy.","explain":"Saying you can improve with practice is growth mindset. Being cheerful is not the same thing, and the last one is fixed mindset."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Lily has missed the netball hoop {n} times. Which plan shows she will persevere wisely?","choices":["Stop and never play again","Try a new way, like bending her knees more","Throw harder the same way"],"answer":1,"hint":"Persevering means keeping going and trying new ways.","explain":"After {n} misses, keeping going with a new way gives her the best chance. Trying the same thing again helps much less.","params":{"n":[3,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"A dog barks suddenly and Jay's worry shoots up. What is the barking?","choices":["A trigger","Self-talk","A growth mindset"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which word means the thing that sets off a big feeling?","explain":"The barking set off Jay's worry, so it is the trigger."}],"explain":{"kid":["Things go wrong for everyone. A setback is something going wrong that slows you down for now. It is not the end. Sam does not pass his swimming badge. That is a setback. Resilience means bouncing back after a setback. Resilient people still get upset. They just get back up.","A trigger is the thing that sets off a big feeling. A boy laughs at Sam. That is the trigger. Distress is a feeling so big that you cannot think. To regulate means to bring the feeling down until you can think again. Slow breaths help. So does saying the name of the feeling. The feeling does not have to go to zero.","Self-talk is the words you say to yourself in your head. A fixed mindset says, \"I am just bad at this. I cannot change.\" A growth mindset says, \"I cannot do it yet. Practice will help.\" Growth mindset is not just being cheerful. It means you believe you can get better.","To persevere means to keep going when it is hard. Doing the same thing again and again helps a little. Trying a new way, or asking for help, helps much more. Every setback is a chance to use all these words."],"adult":["This topic gives children aged 7 to 10 the words for hard moments: resilience, setback, trigger, distress, regulate, self-talk, growth mindset, fixed mindset and persevere. A clear word helps a child notice what is going on. Then they can choose what to do next.","The lab follows one story. Learners drag each word onto the moment it names. They turn a distress meter down with tools that work. They swap fixed thoughts for growth ones. Then they pick a plan each week so Sam can reach his swimming badge after a setback.","Common mistakes: thinking resilience means never getting upset, or that to regulate means to make a feeling vanish or hide it. Many children think growth mindset just means being positive. It means believing you can get better with practice and new ways. Some think to persevere is to repeat the same thing. Children also mix up a trigger, which sets off a feeling, with a setback, which is the thing going wrong.","At home, use the words after real events, and use them about yourself too. Praise the way your child worked, such as trying a new way or asking for help, rather than saying they are clever."]},"familyTip":"After a hard moment, use the words together: what was the setback, what was the trigger, how did you regulate, and what will you try next time? Share one setback of your own too.","keyIdea":"Resilience is bouncing back after a setback. A setback is something going wrong that slows you down for now. A trigger is what sets off a big feeling, and distress is a feeling so big you cannot think. To regulate is to bring that feeling down until you can think, not to make it vanish. Self-talk is what you say to yourself in your head. A growth mindset says you can get better with practice; a fixed mindset says you cannot change. To persevere is to keep going, trying new ways.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Growth mindset or fixed mindset? Ask: does this thought say I can get better?","opts":{"bins":["Growth mindset","Fixed mindset"],"items":[["I can't do it yet",0],["Mistakes show me what to change",0],["Practice makes my brain stronger",0],["I'm just not a maths person",1],["If I were clever it would be easy",1],["I'll never get better",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagbounce","caption":"Flagship lab: put the resilience words on Sam's story, turn a distress meter down, flip fixed thoughts to growth ones and help Sam bounce back to his badge.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_0ewYhTSHtP":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is the correct order of a habit loop?","choices":["Cue, routine, reward","Reward, cue, routine","Routine, reward, cue"],"answer":0,"hint":"What comes first and starts the habit?","explain":"A cue triggers the routine, and the reward makes your brain want to repeat it."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Ella wants to drink more water. Which plan is best?","choices":["Fill a bottle each night and leave it by her school bag","Try to remember when she is thirsty","Wait until she is in the mood"],"answer":0,"hint":"Link it to a clear cue and make it easy to start.","explain":"Leaving a full bottle by her bag gives a clear cue and makes drinking water the easy choice."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Sam missed his new habit on day {d}. What should he do?","choices":["Carry on the next day: one miss barely matters","Start again from day 1","Give up, because the habit is ruined"],"answer":0,"hint":"What did the 2010 study find about missing one day?","explain":"Missing day {d} hardly changes how automatic the habit gets. Carrying on is what counts.","params":{"d":[5,40]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Children who loved drawing were promised a prize for drawing. Later, with no prize, what happened?","choices":["They chose to draw less than children who got no prize","They drew more than ever","Nothing changed"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can an outside reward crowd out an inside one?","explain":"The expected prize made drawing feel like work, so they drew less later. This is called the overjustification effect."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Why do people often put off tasks they care about?","choices":["To escape an uncomfortable feeling now","Because they are lazy","Because they cannot tell the time"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of procrastination as a mood problem, not a time problem.","explain":"Delaying gives quick relief from feeling bored, worried or unsure. That is why it is a mood problem, not laziness."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"A club lets members choose projects, sets tasks they can just manage and works in teams. Which needs does it meet?","choices":["Autonomy, competence and relatedness","Only competence","None, because there are no prizes"],"answer":0,"hint":"Choice, skill and connection: which of these are there?","explain":"Choosing projects is autonomy, tasks they can manage build competence, and teams give relatedness."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Noah's parents offer £{n} for every hour he spends on his favourite hobby, drawing. What is a risk?","choices":["He may start drawing for the money and enjoy it less","Nothing, because rewards always help","He will want to draw forever"],"answer":0,"hint":"What can an outside reward do to an inside reason?","explain":"Paying £{n} for something he already loves can turn it into a job, so he may enjoy it less when the money stops.","params":{"n":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Zoe has {d} days until a project is due and keeps avoiding it. Which first step helps most?","choices":["Work on it for just two minutes now","Wait until she feels ready","Plan to do it all the night before"],"answer":0,"hint":"What shrinks the bad feeling that causes delay?","explain":"Starting for two minutes makes the task feel doable and shrinks the dread. Waiting lets the feeling grow over {d} days.","params":{"d":[3,10]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Each time Leah sits on the bus, she puts her earphones in and plays a game. What is the cue in this habit?","choices":["Sitting on the bus","Playing the game","Enjoying the game"],"answer":0,"hint":"The cue is the trigger that comes first.","explain":"Sitting on the bus is the cue. Playing is the routine and the fun is the reward."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Omar wants to read for {n} minutes each night but keeps scrolling instead. Which change helps most?","choices":["Put the book on his pillow and charge the phone in another room","Promise himself he will try harder","Wait until he feels like reading"],"answer":0,"hint":"Make the good habit easy and the bad one awkward.","explain":"With the book on his pillow and the phone out of reach, {n} minutes of reading is the easiest choice on a tired night. Willpower alone often fails.","params":{"n":[10,30]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Priya has done her new morning stretch for {d} days. It still does not feel automatic. What is most likely?","choices":["That is normal: habits often take about two months or more","She is doing it wrong","Habits always form in 21 days, so she has failed"],"answer":0,"hint":"How long did the middle person take in the 2010 study?","explain":"After {d} days a habit often still needs effort. In the study the middle time was 66 days, from 18 to 254, so she should keep going.","params":{"d":[20,40]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Which is an inside (intrinsic) reason for playing the piano?","choices":["She loves how the music sounds","Her parents pay her","She wants to avoid being told off"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is the reward the activity itself, or something outside it?","explain":"Loving the sound is an inside reason: the playing is its own reward. Money and avoiding trouble are outside reasons."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Jay has an essay due in {d} days. Every time he opens it he feels worried, so he watches videos instead. What is really going on?","choices":["He is escaping a bad feeling for quick relief","He has too little time","He does not care about school"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what changes in how he feels when he delays.","explain":"Watching videos gives quick relief from the worry. With {d} days left, the feeling will come back bigger. Starting for two minutes can shrink it.","params":{"d":[2,7]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A habit is something you do almost without thinking. Every habit runs on a loop. First comes a cue, a trigger such as your phone buzzing or getting home from school. Then the routine, the thing you do, such as checking the app or grabbing a snack. Last comes the reward, the good feeling, which teaches your brain to repeat the routine next time the cue appears.","Willpower is weakest when you are tired, hungry or stressed. So change your surroundings instead. Put the guitar on a stand where you can see it, and charge your phone in another room. If a good habit takes one easy step to start and a bad habit takes several awkward steps, the good one wins on tired nights. New habits take time. You may have heard 21 days, but that is a myth. In a 2010 study, the middle person took 66 days for a daily habit to feel automatic, and some took 254. Missing one day hardly made a difference, so if you slip, just carry on tomorrow.","Motivation can come from inside or outside. Inside (intrinsic) motivation means you do it because you enjoy it or care about it. Outside (extrinsic) motivation means you do it for a prize, money or to avoid trouble. Prizes can help with dull jobs, but paying someone for something they already love can make them enjoy it less. People also keep going when they feel three things: choice (autonomy), getting better at it (competence) and being with others (relatedness).","Putting things off is not laziness. Usually a task makes you feel bored, worried or unsure, and delaying it gives quick relief. The bad feeling comes back bigger as the deadline gets closer. Starting for just two minutes often shrinks the feeling."],"adult":["This topic is for ages 12 to 13. It covers how habits form and what keeps people motivated. A habit is a loop of cue, routine and reward. It becomes automatic when it is repeated in the same setting. In a 2010 study, Lally and colleagues asked people to take up one daily habit. The middle time to reach 95% of their top automatic level was 66 days. The range was 18 to 254 days. Missing one day did not set people back. The popular 21-day rule has no good evidence.","Good habit design uses cues and friction. Tie the new habit to a cue that already happens, make it easy to start, and put barriers in front of the habits you want to drop. This works better than willpower, which is weakest when people are tired or stressed. In the lab, each evening compares Ravi's energy with the effort it takes to start.","Intrinsic motivation means doing something because you value it. Extrinsic motivation means doing it for a reward or to avoid a punishment. Rewards can help people start dull tasks. But an expected prize for something a child already enjoys can make them choose it less later. This is the overjustification effect. Lepper, Greene and Nisbett showed it in 1973 with young children who were promised a certificate for drawing. Self-determination theory (Ryan and Deci, 2000) says motivation lasts when three needs are met: autonomy, competence and relatedness.","Common mistakes: seeing procrastination as poor time management, when it is mostly a quick fix for a bad mood (Sirois and Pychyl, 2013); believing habits form in exactly 21 days; giving up after one missed day; and thinking more rewards always mean more motivation. Help young people notice the feeling that makes them delay. Then agree a two-minute start on the task."]},"familyTip":"Pick one small habit to build together, like a ten-minute tidy after dinner. Agree on the cue that starts it, make it easy to begin, and track it on a calendar for two months. If someone misses a day, just carry on the next day.","keyIdea":"A habit is a loop: a cue triggers a routine, and a reward makes your brain want to repeat it. You build good habits by making them easy to start and linking them to a clear cue. Making bad habits awkward helps more than relying on willpower. Habits take weeks to feel automatic, often about two months, and one missed day barely matters. Inside (intrinsic) motivation, doing something because you value it, lasts longer than outside rewards and punishments. Putting things off is mostly a way to escape a bad feeling, so starting small helps. People keep going when they feel choice, skill and connection.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Inside or outside reason? Ask: is the activity its own reward?","opts":{"bins":["Inside reason","Outside reason"],"items":[["Reading because the story is gripping",0],["Practising because you love the sound",0],["Running because it clears your head",0],["Revising to avoid a detention",1],["Tidying for pocket money",1],["Training only to win a medal",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flaghabit","caption":"Flagship lab: build habit loops, redesign a room, drag the real habit curve, sort reasons, watch procrastination's mood swing and meet three needs.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_Iwg2diBSyW":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Jo's sister knocks her tower over {n} times. Jo feels angry. Is it OK to feel angry?","choices":["Yes, then calm your body","No, never","Yes, so you can hit"],"answer":0,"hint":"Feelings are OK. What matters is what you do with them.","explain":"Being knocked over {n} times would make anyone angry. All feelings are OK. Jo can use a calm tool, but hitting is never OK.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"You feel upset at school. Where is a good quiet space?","choices":["The calm corner in class","Out of the gate, alone","The noisy hall"],"answer":0,"hint":"A good quiet space is safe, and a grown-up knows where you are.","explain":"The calm corner is quiet and your teacher can see you. Going off alone is not safe, and noise keeps you buzzing."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Your little brother takes your crayon. You feel your face get hot. When should you use a calm tool?","choices":["Now, while the feeling is small","Only when it gets huge","Never"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is a small storm or a huge storm easier to shrink?","explain":"A hot face is an early clue. A small feeling is easier to calm, so use a tool as soon as you notice it."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Why do calm tools help?","choices":["They help your body settle so you can think","They make the problem vanish","They win the game for you"],"answer":0,"hint":"After you calm down, is the problem still there? What can you do now?","explain":"A calm body can think clearly. The problem is still there, but now you can work out how to fix it."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"The party is very loud and Sam feels too much. Which tool fits best?","choices":["Go to a quiet room with a grown-up","Shout louder than the music","Stay by the big speaker"],"answer":0,"hint":"The noise keeps going. Which tool gets Sam away from it?","explain":"When noise is the trouble, a quiet space works best. Sam goes with a grown-up so he is safe."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Lily took {n} slow breaths but still feels a bit cross. What can she try?","choices":["Count to ten slowly","Throw her bag","Hold her breath"],"answer":0,"hint":"If one calm tool is not enough, you can add another calm tool.","explain":"After {n} breaths, Lily can add a second tool, like counting slowly. Holding her breath or throwing things makes it worse.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"What comes first when a big feeling starts?","choices":["Notice it, then use a calm tool","Fix the problem straight away","Shout so it comes out"],"answer":0,"hint":"It is hard to fix a problem while the storm is big.","explain":"First notice the feeling and calm your body. Then you can think and fix the problem."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Your tower falls down and you feel cross. What is a calm tool you can use?","choices":["Take slow breaths","Kick the blocks","Shout at your friend"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one helps your body settle, and does not hurt anyone?","explain":"Slow breaths calm your body. Kicking or shouting makes the storm bigger and can hurt someone."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Max counts to ten, but he rushes and finishes in just {n} seconds. Does fast counting help him calm down?","choices":["Not much, count slowly","Yes, faster is better","Only if he shouts"],"answer":0,"hint":"Counting helps because of the wait. What happens to the wait when you go fast?","explain":"Counting works because it gives your body time to settle. Rushing to ten in {n} seconds makes the pause too short. Slow counts, like big steps, work better.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"How do you breathe out in balloon breaths?","choices":["Slowly, like cooling hot soup","In quick puffs","You hold it in"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of blowing on hot soup to cool it, gently and for a long time.","explain":"A slow, long breath out tells your body it is safe, so your heart slows down and the storm gets smaller."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Ella feels upset. She takes {n} slow breaths and feels calm. What can she do next?","choices":["Say how she feels","Pretend nothing happened","Hit someone"],"answer":0,"hint":"Calming helps you think. The problem is still there, so what can you do about it?","explain":"After {n} slow breaths, Ella can think clearly. Now she can use her words to say how she feels or ask for help.","params":{"n":[2,5]}}],"explain":{"kid":["When you lose a game or someone grabs your toy, a big feeling can come. It is like a storm inside. Your heart goes fast. Your hands get tight. All feelings are OK. You can make the storm smaller.","Tool one is balloon breaths. Breathe in through your nose, like you fill a balloon. Then let the air out slowly, like you cool hot soup. Fast puffs do not help. Slow air out tells your body it is safe.","Tool two is to count to ten. Say each number slowly, like big steps. The wait gives your body time to settle. Tool three is a quiet space, like a calm corner. Tell a grown-up where you go.","Hitting and shouting make the storm bigger. After you calm down, the problem is still there. But now you can think. You can say how you feel, or ask for help."],"adult":["At five to seven, children start to learn that they can do something about a big feeling instead of being carried along by it. This topic gives them three concrete tools to practise: slow breathing with a long out-breath, counting slowly to ten, and moving to a safe, quiet space. They also learn the simple reason these work: the body settles, and a settled body can think again.","Why it works: an upset sets off the body's stress response, so the heart speeds up and breathing gets quick and shallow. A slow, long breath out engages the calming side of the nervous system and lowers the heart rate. Counting slowly adds a pause before acting, and a quiet place removes noise and other triggers that keep the alarm going.","Common mistakes to watch for: puffing out fast or holding the breath instead of breathing out slowly; racing through the count to get it over with; running off alone, which is not safe; using hitting or shouting as a way to let it out; and expecting the problem to vanish once calm. Be careful not to tell a child to stop feeling. The aim is to calm the body, not to ban the feeling.","In the lab your child shrinks a feelings storm with three slow balloon breaths and sees fast puffs make it grow, counts ten stepping stones at a slow pace, drags Mia to two safe, quiet places, picks a calm tool for three upset friends, and puts the steps in order: big feeling, notice it, use a calm tool, body settles, then fix it."]},"familyTip":"Practise when everyone is calm, not in the middle of a meltdown. At bedtime, do three balloon breaths together: in through the nose, then out slowly like cooling soup. Agree on a calm spot at home. Next time a big feeling starts, say, \"I see a storm coming. Balloon breaths or calm spot?\"","keyIdea":"A big feeling is like a storm inside your body. You can make it smaller with a calm tool: breathe in, then let the air out slowly; count to ten slowly; or go to a safe, quiet place where a grown-up knows you are. Use a tool as soon as you notice the feeling. Calming does not make the problem go away, but it helps you think, so then you can fix it.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Calm tool or not? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["🎈 Calms me","⚡ Makes the storm bigger"],"items":[["Slow breaths out",0],["Count to ten slowly",0],["Go to the calm corner",0],["Hug my teddy",0],["Hit my brother",1],["Shout at my friend",1],["Kick the door",1],["Throw the game",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagcalm","caption":"Flagship lab: shrink a feelings storm with slow breaths, count ten slow stones, find a quiet place, pick calm tools, then calm first and fix it.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_j8Pv3s7TZR":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which is true about coping tools?","choices":["Different feelings need different tools","One tool works for every feeling","Only grown-ups use them"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does a tool that cools anger also give comfort to sadness?","explain":"Hot anger needs cooling and heavy sadness needs comfort. So match the tool to the feeling. Everyone uses coping tools."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"It rains on sports day. Mia can't change the rain. What helps?","choices":["Find a fun thing to do inside","Try to make the rain stop","Stay cross all day"],"answer":0,"hint":"If you can't change a problem, what can you change instead?","explain":"No one can stop the rain. Mia can help her feelings: say it is a let-down, then find something fun to do."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Sam tried {n} slow breaths, but he still feels lonely. What next?","choices":["Try a different tool, like calling a friend","Give up, nothing works","Do the breaths louder"],"answer":0,"hint":"When a tool does not work, is it a clue to try another?","explain":"Sam's {n} breaths calmed his body, but loneliness needs company. A tool that does not work is a clue to try one that fits.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Leo is worried about a spelling test tomorrow. He is at home. What fits best?","choices":["Practise the words with Mum","Ask his teacher right now","Stomp about the house"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can Leo change this problem? Who is with him at home?","explain":"Leo can do something about the test, and Mum is at home. Practising the words makes the worry smaller."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Jay is boiling mad in the playground. Where can he cool off?","choices":["The calm corner in class","His bedroom","The sofa at home"],"answer":0,"hint":"Jay is at school. Which of these places is at school?","explain":"His bedroom and the sofa are at home. At school, the calm corner is a safe place to cool off, and a teacher knows where he is."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Zoe has been cross for {n} minutes because her brother broke her model. She is calm now. What next?","choices":["Tell him how she feels","Break his toy back","Pretend it never happened"],"answer":0,"hint":"Once your body is cool, you can think. What can Zoe do about the problem now?","explain":"After {n} minutes, Zoe is calm, so now she can talk. Telling her brother how she feels can fix things. Breaking his toy makes it worse.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which question helps you choose a coping tool?","choices":["How do I feel, and where am I?","What is my favourite colour?","Who is the tallest?"],"answer":0,"hint":"A good tool fits the feeling and the place.","explain":"Ask: How do I feel? Where am I? Can I change it? The answers point to the tool that fits."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Ella is so angry her face is hot. What should she do first?","choices":["Cool off with slow breaths","Argue straight away","Curl up and nap"],"answer":0,"hint":"Anger is hot and fast. Which tool cools a hot body down?","explain":"Slow breaths cool a hot, fast body. Arguing while boiling makes words come out hot, so cool off first and talk after."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Tom has felt sad for {n} days since his dog went to live on a farm. What could help?","choices":["Talk to someone he trusts","Squeeze a stress ball","Run laps until he is tired"],"answer":0,"hint":"Sadness is heavy and slow. Does it need cooling down, or comfort?","explain":"After {n} sad days, Tom needs comfort and company. Talking to someone he trusts helps. A stress ball suits hot anger, not heavy sadness.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Ava feels worried in class. Which tool can she use there?","choices":["Tell her teacher","Go to her bedroom","Hug her dog"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about where Ava is right now. Which tool is there with her?","explain":"Ava is at school, so she needs a school tool. Her teacher is there. Her bedroom and her dog are at home."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Ben lost his pencil {n} minutes before a test. What kind of problem is this?","choices":["One he can fix: ask to borrow one","One he can't change","One to cry about all day"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can Ben do something to make this problem go away?","explain":"With {n} minutes left, Ben can fix it. He can look in his bag or ask to borrow a pencil. Fixable problems need a plan.","params":{"n":[2,5]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A coping tool helps you with a big feeling. But one tool does not fit every feeling. Think of a toolbox. You would not fix a bike with a spoon.","Anger feels hot and fast. Your face gets hot and your fists get tight. So first cool your body down. Take slow breaths, walk away, or squeeze a ball.","Sadness feels heavy and slow. You want to curl up. Sad feelings need comfort. Talk to someone you trust, hug a pet, or do a small thing you like.","Where you are matters too. At school you can't go to your room, but you can tell your teacher. Some problems you can fix, like a lost pencil. Some you can't, like rain. Then you help your feelings instead. If a tool does not work, try a new one."],"adult":["At seven to nine, children already know a few calming tools. The next step is choosing between them. This topic teaches that coping strategies are not one-size-fits-all: the best choice depends on the emotion, the setting, and whether the problem can be changed.","Psychologists split coping into two families. Problem-focused coping changes the situation, such as finding the lost pencil or asking to practise for a test. Emotion-focused coping changes how you feel when the situation cannot be changed, such as talking to a friend about a move or finding something fun on a rainy sports day. High-energy feelings like anger respond best to tools that lower arousal first; low-energy feelings like sadness respond best to comfort and connection.","Common mistakes to watch for: using one favourite tool for everything; trying to talk a problem through while still boiling with anger; picking a home tool at school; trying to fix the unfixable, which ends in frustration; and giving up after the first tool fails. Be careful not to treat a strategy that failed as proof that nothing works. It is useful information about the fit.","In the lab your child sorts six tools into an anger jar and a sadness jar, switches between school and home to find tools that work in each place, sorts problems into fixable and not fixable, helps Sam when slow breaths only half help, and then makes feeling, place and tool plans for three children."]},"familyTip":"Make a small coping card together with two columns, School and Home, and three rows: angry, sad and worried. Fill in one tool for each box. After the next upset, ask, \"Which tool did you use? Did it fit? What could you try next time?\"","keyIdea":"A coping strategy is a tool for a big feeling. Different feelings need different tools: hot, fast anger needs cooling down, and heavy sadness needs comfort and company. The place matters too, because some tools only work at home or at school. Ask three questions: How do I feel? Where am I? Can I change the problem? If a tool does not work, that is a clue, so try one that fits better.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which feeling does each tool fit best? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["😠 Cools anger","😢 Comforts sadness"],"items":[["Slow, deep breaths",0],["Walk away and cool off",0],["Squeeze a stress ball",0],["Count to ten",0],["Talk to someone you trust",1],["Hug a pet",1],["Listen to a song you like",1],["Ask a friend to play",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagcope","caption":"Flagship lab: sort tools by feeling, switch school and home, sort fixable problems, help Sam try a better tool, then plan.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_miGrca8zaS":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Why does breaking a job into steps help?","choices":["Each step is small enough to start","It makes the job longer","You can skip the hard part"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about how it feels to start a giant job.","explain":"Each step is small enough to start, so you are not stuck looking at the whole job."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which is a small, clear step?","choices":["Read the first 3 spellings out loud","Get better at spelling","Learn all the spellings"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can you tell exactly when it is done?","explain":"Read the first 3 spellings out loud. You know what to do and when it is finished."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"A step still feels too big. What should you do?","choices":["Break it into a smaller step","Give up","Do it all at once"],"answer":0,"hint":"You can cut a step up again.","explain":"Break it into a smaller step, like the first sentence instead of the whole story."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"You finish step 1 of 4. What is a helpful thing to say?","choices":["One done! On to step 2.","Three left. Hopeless.","That does not count."],"answer":0,"hint":"Notice what you did, not only what is left.","explain":"One done! On to step 2. Noticing progress gives you energy for the next step."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Your job has {a} steps. You do 1 step a day. How many days will it take?","answer":"a","hint":"One step each day, so count one day for every step.","explain":"{a} days: one step a day for {a} steps.","params":{"a":[3,8]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"You split a {=s*k} minute job into {k} steps of the same size. How many minutes is each step?","answer":"s","hint":"Share the minutes equally between the steps.","explain":"{s} minutes. {=s*k} minutes shared into {k} equal steps is {s} minutes each.","params":{"s":[5,10],"k":[2,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Your book report is due Friday. It is Monday. What is the best plan?","choices":["Read a bit each day, then write","Do it all on Thursday night","Write it before reading"],"answer":0,"hint":"Spread small steps out, in a sensible order.","explain":"Read a bit each day, then write. Leaving it all to one night makes one giant step."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which is a good first step for tidying your room?","choices":["Put the clothes in the wash basket","Tidy everything at once","Wait until tomorrow"],"answer":0,"hint":"A good step is small, and you can tell when it is done.","explain":"Put the clothes in the wash basket. It is small and clear, so you can start right now."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A project has {a} steps. You finished {b}. How many steps are left?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Take the steps you finished away from all the steps.","explain":"{=a-b} steps are left. You already did {b}, and that progress counts.","params":{"a":[5,9],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"What should come first when you make a poster about frogs?","choices":["Find some facts about frogs","Colour it in","Check the spellings"],"answer":0,"hint":"What do you need before you can write anything?","explain":"Find some facts first. You cannot write about frogs until you know about them. Colouring and checking come last."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"You read {n} pages a day. How many days to read a {=n*d} page book?","answer":"d","hint":"Share the pages out into equal daily steps.","explain":"{d} days. {=n*d} pages split into steps of {n} pages a day takes {d} days.","params":{"n":[2,5],"d":[3,6]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Some jobs feel too big. Tidy your whole room? A poster for school? It is hard to know where to start.","So cut the job up. Clothes in the basket. Books on the shelf. Toys in the box. Each one is a small step.","A good step is small and clear. You know what to do, and you know when it is done. 'Get better at spelling' is not a step. 'Say three words out loud' is.","Do the first step now. If it still feels too big, cut it again. When a step is done, say 'One done!' and go on to the next."],"adult":["At seven to nine, children start to get longer pieces of work: projects, reading logs, practice for a show. The PSHE Association programme of study for Key Stage 2 includes setting goals, breaking them into steps you can achieve, and noticing progress. Psychologists call this skill chunking. It is part of planning, which is still growing at this age.","Children get stuck when the whole job sits in front of them as one block. The first step should be small enough to start within a minute, like 'find three facts about frogs', not a wish like 'do a good poster'. Steps also need a sensible order: facts before writing, plan before drawing, checking last.","Common mistakes: steps that are just the whole job said again ('finish the story'), vague steps that can never be ticked off ('get better at maths'), leaving it all to the last night, and counting only what is left instead of what is done. Waiting until you feel ready is the classic slip. Starting a small step is what makes you feel ready.","In the lab your child chips a big job into four steps, orders the steps of a frog poster, sorts steps into small and clear or too big and fuzzy, splits steps that are still too big, and crosses stepping stones while saying a progress check after each one."]},"familyTip":"Next time a job feels big, write each step on a sticky note together. Stick them on the door in order, and let your child pull one off each time a step is done. Say 'one done!' out loud.","keyIdea":"A big job feels hard to start because you see all of it at once. Break it into small, clear steps, put them in a sensible order, and start the first one now. If a step still feels too big, break it again. After each step, notice your progress: one done, on to the next.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is it a good small step? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["✓ Small and clear","✗ Too big or fuzzy"],"items":[["Put my socks in the drawer",0],["Write the title of my poster",0],["Read page 1 of my book",0],["Say the 2 times table once",0],["Tidy everything",1],["Be good at reading",1],["Finish the whole project tonight",1],["Do some stuff",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagsteps","caption":"Flagship lab: chip a big job into steps, order them, sort small and clear steps, split big ones and cross the stones.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_cJjnPjuvCU":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What turns a wish into a goal?","choices":["Saying exactly what, how you will measure it and by when","Wanting it very much","Telling all your friends"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think specific, measurable and time frame.","explain":"A goal is specific, measurable and has a time frame, so you can plan for it and check it."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which goal has no time frame?","choices":["Swim 50 m backstroke","Swim 50 m backstroke by July","Swim 50 m backstroke in 6 weeks"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for words that say by when, like a date or a number of weeks.","explain":"\"Swim 50 m backstroke\" is specific and measurable, but it says nothing about when."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Sam can juggle {n} times. Which goal is too big for this month?","choices":["{=n*50} times","{=n+4} times","{=n+8} times"],"answer":0,"hint":"Too big means so hard you may give up.","explain":"{=n*50} is far beyond {n} in a month, so it is likely to feel hopeless. {=n+4} or {=n+8} is a stretch.","params":{"n":[3,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Obstacle: \"My little brother wants to play when I do homework.\" Which is the if–then plan?","choices":["If he asks, then I say I will play after 20 minutes","I will try to ignore him","Homework is too hard"],"answer":0,"hint":"An if–then plan says: if this happens, then I will do this exact thing.","explain":"It names the obstacle and a clear action. \"Try to ignore\" is vague and the last one is not a plan."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Why track your progress each week instead of only at the deadline?","choices":["So you spot problems while there is time to fix them","So the goal gets bigger","You do not need to"],"answer":0,"hint":"What can you do if you find out you are behind early?","explain":"Weekly checks show when you fall behind, so you can change the plan in time."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"You planned {k} maths problems a day. You have done that every day this week. What does that show?","choices":["You are on track","You are behind","The goal is too big"],"answer":0,"hint":"Compare what you did with the plan.","explain":"Doing {k} a day, as planned, means you are on track. Keep going, and review at the end of the next week.","params":{"k":[3,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Your times-table scores have not gone up for 2 weeks. What should you do first?","choices":["Find which facts you miss and practise those","Give up times tables","Do the same test again and again"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find out why it is not working.","explain":"Look for the weak part and change how you practise. Repeating the same thing gives the same result."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"When is it right to make a goal smaller?","choices":["When it is too big to fit your life, even with a good plan","As soon as it feels hard","Never"],"answer":0,"hint":"Feeling hard is normal for a stretch.","explain":"A stretch should feel hard. Shrink the goal only if it is not realistic, for example if it leaves no time for other things."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which is a specific, measurable goal with a time frame?","choices":["Score 18 out of 20 on my spelling test by the end of the month","Get better at spelling","Do lots of spelling one day"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can you check it with a number and a date?","explain":"18 out of 20 is measurable, spelling test is specific, and the end of the month is the time frame. The others are wishes."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Ava can do {n} skips in a row. Which goal is a good stretch for next week?","choices":["{=n+5} skips in a row","{n} skips in a row","{=n*20} skips in a row"],"answer":0,"hint":"A stretch is a bit harder than now, but realistic.","explain":"{=n+5} is harder than {n} but reachable in a week. {n} is no stretch, and {=n*20} is too big for now.","params":{"n":[5,20]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which is an if–then plan for an obstacle?","choices":["If I am tired after school, then I practise for just 5 minutes","I will try really hard","I hope nothing goes wrong"],"answer":0,"hint":"It names the problem and what you will do about it.","explain":"\"If tired, then 5 minutes\" decides the action before the problem comes. Trying hard and hoping are not plans."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Your plan is {p} pages a week. After 2 weeks you have read {=2*p-20} pages. Are you on track?","choices":["Behind by 20 pages","On track","Ahead of plan"],"answer":0,"hint":"The plan for 2 weeks is 2 × {p}.","explain":"The plan for 2 weeks is {=2*p} pages and you have read {=2*p-20}. You are 20 pages behind, so it is time to check why.","params":{"p":[30,80]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"You keep missing your piano practice because you do it late at night when you are sleepy. What is the best change?","choices":["Move practice to before dinner","Give up piano","Keep the same time and try harder"],"answer":0,"hint":"The goal is fine. What is the real problem?","explain":"The problem is the time, so change the plan. Giving up drops the goal, and the same plan gives the same result."}],"explain":{"kid":["\"Get better at swimming\" is a wish. You can never tell when you have done it. A goal is sharper: \"Swim 25 metres front crawl without stopping by the end of term.\" It is specific (what exactly), measurable (a number you can check) and has a time frame (by when).","Pick a goal that is a stretch. It should be harder than what you do now, but not so big that you give up. If you read 2 books a month, 3 or 4 is a stretch. Twenty is too big for now.","Things will get in your way, so plan for them. Use an if–then plan: \"If it rains on my running day, then I will skip indoors for 10 minutes.\" Then track your progress each week. Write down what you did and compare it with your plan.","If you fall behind, do not give up. Ask why. Then change the plan: a new time, extra practice on the hard part, or a smaller step. Only make the goal smaller if it really is too big."],"adult":["Children aged 9 to 11 learn to set realistic personal goals, plan steps, check progress and adjust. This sits in the IB PYP Personal, Social and Physical Education strand (identity) and in UK PSHE work on self-esteem and aspiration. Useful terms are specific, measurable, realistic (achievable) and time frame, often taught as SMART. Add short-term and long-term goals, steps, obstacles, progress and review.","Research backs three habits. Specific, fairly hard goals lead to more effort than \"do your best\" (Locke and Latham's goal-setting theory). If–then plans, called implementation intentions (Gollwitzer), make children far more likely to act when an obstacle comes. And regular progress checks, best when written down, help people stick with a goal (Harkin and colleagues, 2016).","Watch for common mistakes. Goals are vague (\"be better at maths\"), too big (\"every times table by tomorrow\") or have no date. There is no plan for obstacles. Progress is checked only at the deadline, and a slow week is treated as failure. The other slip is giving up the goal when it is the plan that needs to change. Model saying: \"That didn't work. What will I try instead?\"","In the lab your child sharpens a vague goal into a specific, measurable one with a time frame. They set a goal size in the stretch zone, match obstacles to if–then plans, log weeks of reading against a plan line, and choose the best change when progress stalls."]},"familyTip":"Pick one goal together for the next four weeks, such as a skill or a book series. Write it as \"I will ___ by ___\", add one if–then plan, and put a simple tick chart on the fridge. Check it every Sunday and ask: \"On track? If not, what will we change?\"","keyIdea":"A wish is \"I want to get better\". A goal says exactly what you will do, how you will measure it, and by when, and it is a stretch you can really reach. Plan the steps and what you will do when something gets in the way, track your progress against the plan, and when it stops working, change the plan before you give up on the goal.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is each one a sharp goal or a vague wish? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["🎯 Sharp goal","💭 Vague wish"],"items":[["Read 3 chapter books by half term",0],["Learn my 8 times table by Friday",0],["Run 1 km without stopping by May",0],["Get better at football",1],["Be good at drawing one day",1],["Practise piano 10 minutes a day for 4 weeks",0],["Be the best at everything",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flaggoal","caption":"Flagship lab: sharpen a vague goal, set a stretch-size goal, match obstacles to if–then plans, track weeks against a plan and choose how to adjust.","opts":{"step":0}}}});
